Honor SYSROOT and SYSTEM_PROCESSOR env vars for CMake's own cross detection

run_cmake_configure() already reads CC/CXX from the environment so a cross
compiler wrapper can be swapped in, but never tells CMake's own
find_package()/find_path()/find_library() calls to look under a sysroot -
those search hardcoded host paths (e.g. CheckX11 in SDL3's sdlchecks.cmake
walks /usr/include/X11, /usr/X11R6/include, ...) regardless of what
--sysroot the compiler itself was given. Under a buildroot-style cross
build this means SDL3 configures against nothing (X11/Wayland dev files
live only in the target sysroot, never on the host build machine), and
CMake's own message is misleading: "SDL could not find X11 or Wayland
development libraries on your system", when they are simply not where
CMake is looking.

Read SYSROOT the same way CC/CXX already are, and when it's set, add the
standard CMAKE_FIND_ROOT_PATH* defines: LIBRARY/INCLUDE/PACKAGE restricted
to the sysroot (host libs/configs must never leak into a cross build), and
PROGRAM forced to NEVER - CMake's default here is BOTH, not NEVER, since
this script never sets CMAKE_SYSTEM_NAME/PROCESSOR on Linux and so CMake
never considers itself to be cross-compiling. Without forcing PROGRAM off,
find_package(Python3) picks the sysroot's own /usr/bin/python3 - a
target-arch ELF (e.g. aarch64 on an x86_64 build host) - and fails to
execute it, rather than falling through to the host's usable interpreter
on PATH.

Also read SYSTEM_PROCESSOR and, when set, add CMAKE_SYSTEM_NAME/PROCESSOR.
Without it CMAKE_SYSTEM_PROCESSOR is whatever this script itself runs on
(the build host), not the compiler's actual target -
src/xenia/base/xe_target.cc's XE_TARGET_AARCH64/XE_TARGET_X86_64 detection
reads CMAKE_SYSTEM_PROCESSOR at configure time, so on an aarch64 target
build this silently selects the x64/Xbyak CPU backend instead of
a64/xbyak_aarch64: it configures cleanly and only fails later, deep in
backend/x64, on x86 inline asm an aarch64-targeting clang cannot compile.
Setting CMAKE_SYSTEM_NAME is also what flips CMAKE_CROSSCOMPILING to
TRUE, which is what makes FIND_ROOT_PATH_MODE_PROGRAM's real default
NEVER instead of BOTH - the explicit NEVER above is kept regardless, belt
and braces.

Signed-off-by: Carlos Martins <cjom@reglinux.org>

--- a/xenia-build.py
+++ b/xenia-build.py
@@ -879,6 +879,43 @@
             f"-DCMAKE_C_COMPILER={c_compiler}",
             f"-DCMAKE_CXX_COMPILER={cxx_compiler}",
         ]
+        sysroot = os.environ.get("SYSROOT")
+        if sysroot:
+            args += [
+                f"-DCMAKE_FIND_ROOT_PATH={sysroot}",
+                "-DCMAKE_FIND_ROOT_PATH_MODE_LIBRARY=ONLY",
+                "-DCMAKE_FIND_ROOT_PATH_MODE_INCLUDE=ONLY",
+                "-DCMAKE_FIND_ROOT_PATH_MODE_PACKAGE=ONLY",
+                # Interpreters/tools (python3, wayland-scanner, ...) must
+                # keep resolving from PATH, i.e. the host build machine -
+                # the sysroot's own /usr/bin/python3 is a target-arch ELF
+                # (e.g. aarch64 on an x86_64 build host) that CMake would
+                # otherwise happily pick and then fail to execute. This is
+                # NOT already CMake's default here: default PROGRAM mode
+                # is BOTH unless CMAKE_CROSSCOMPILING is TRUE, which it
+                # never is in this build (no CMAKE_SYSTEM_NAME/PROCESSOR
+                # is set anywhere in this script on Linux).
+                "-DCMAKE_FIND_ROOT_PATH_MODE_PROGRAM=NEVER",
+            ]
+        system_processor = os.environ.get("SYSTEM_PROCESSOR")
+        if system_processor:
+            # Without this, CMAKE_SYSTEM_PROCESSOR is whatever this script
+            # itself runs on (the build host), not the compiler's actual
+            # target - src/xenia/base/xe_target.cc's XE_TARGET_AARCH64 /
+            # XE_TARGET_X86_64 detection reads CMAKE_SYSTEM_PROCESSOR at
+            # configure time, so on an aarch64 target build this silently
+            # selects the x64/Xbyak CPU backend instead of a64/xbyak_aarch64
+            # - it configures fine and dies later, deep in backend/x64, on
+            # x86 inline asm an aarch64-targeting clang can't compile.
+            # Setting CMAKE_SYSTEM_NAME here is also what flips
+            # CMAKE_CROSSCOMPILING to TRUE, which is what makes
+            # FIND_ROOT_PATH_MODE_PROGRAM's real default NEVER instead of
+            # BOTH - the explicit NEVER above stays anyway, belt and
+            # braces.
+            args += [
+                "-DCMAKE_SYSTEM_NAME=Linux",
+                f"-DCMAKE_SYSTEM_PROCESSOR={system_processor}",
+            ]
     if sys.platform == "darwin" and target_arch is not None:
         # Apple clang is universal; CMAKE_OSX_ARCHITECTURES drives -arch.
         # We don't set CMAKE_SYSTEM_PROCESSOR here — without
